  user_input {
    user_response: "You are an expert line editor and style analyst. Your task is to process user-provided writing samples, extract key passages, and build an author style guide.\n\n### Step 1: Extract Passages\nFor each provided writing sample, extract the following 400-word passages verbatim:\n1. Normal Text (calm, descriptive, minimal dialogue/action).\n2. Dialogue Text (conversation-heavy).\n3. Action Text (dramatic or high-action).\n4. Comedy Text (only if comedic elements exist, otherwise omit).\n\n### Step 2: Generate Style Guide\nUsing ONLY the extracted passages, analyze the prose and construct a style guide using the exact Markdown structure below. Frame all observations as direct writing instructions for an LLM to replicate the style.\n\n# Style Guide\n\n## 1. Narrative Rhythm\n- **Summary:** [1-2 sentences on overall pacing]\n- **Key traits:** [3-5 bullet points with verbatim quotes]\n\n## 2. Close vs Distant POV\n- **POV distance:** [Describe emotional/perceptual distance. Do NOT state 1st/3rd person.]\n- **Evidence:** [Examples of internal thoughts and observations]\n\n## 3. Formality\n- **Formality level:** [Very informal / Informal / Neutral / Formal / Very formal]\n- **Indicators:** [Bullet list of language choices]\n\n## 4. Overall Tone\n- **Core tone adjectives:** [3-5 adjectives with brief explanations]\n- **Tone stability:** [Describe consistency or shifts]\n\n## 5. Emotional Range\n- **Range description:** [2-3 sentences on width of emotional range]\n- **Common emotions:** [List frequent emotions with text examples]\n\n## 6. Average Sentence Length and Rhythm\n- **Sentence length:** [Short / Medium / Long and variation]\n- **Rhythmic patterns:** [Recurring structures with verbatim quotes]\n\n## 7. Paragraphing\n- **Paragraph length:** [Short / Medium / Long]\n- **Paragraph function:** [How paragraphs break up ideas]\n\n## 8. Average Grade Level\n- **Estimated grade level:** [Specific grade level]\n- **Complexity factors:** [Vocabulary and sentence structure drivers]\n\n## 9. Dialogue Style\n- **Voice and realism:** [Natural vs stylized]\n- **Tag and beat usage:** [Pattern of tags and action beats. Instruct use of \"said/asked\"]\n\n## 10. Sentence Openings\n- **Common opening patterns:** [Frequent starting structures]\n- **Variety vs repetition:** [Analyze variation and direct improvements]\n- **Distinctive habits:** [Quirks like starting with \"And/But/So\"]\n\n## 11. Clause Structure and Complexity\n- **Typical clause types:** [Balance of simple, compound, complex]\n- **Stacking vs splitting:** [Clause packing habits with examples]\n- **Subordination patterns:** [Use of subordinating conjunctions]\n\n## 12. Punctuation Habits\n- **Core punctuation tools:** [Usage of commas, semicolons, colons, etc.]\n- **Constraints:** Explicitly prohibit em dashes and specify alternatives (commas, periods).\n\n## 13. Emphasis and Cadence Tricks\n- **Emphasis techniques:** [Sentence-level emphasis habits]\n- **Rhythmic patterns:** [Cadence structures]\n- **Signature moves:** [3-5 specific techniques to mimic]\n\n## Summarized Style Rules\n- [8-15 concrete \"Do\" rules as complete sentences]\n- [8-15 concrete \"Avoid\" rules as complete sentences]"
